{"id": "live-299", "title": "Orchard BJK Company, LLC v. Coolbaugh Township Board of Supervisors (Stormwater-Ordinance Challenge to Warehouse Buffer Requirements)", "category": "zoning-land-use", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-10-15", "jurisdiction": "Commonwealth Court of Pennsylvania", "state": "PA", "amount": "Not a monetary claim; developer sought a declaratory judgment voiding stormwater buffer ordinance amendments as unconstitutional and invalid special legislation", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://storage.courtlistener.com/pdf/2026/10/15/orchard_bjk_co._llc__pocono_mountains_industries_inc._v._coolbaugh_twp..pdf", "summary": "The Commonwealth Court of Pennsylvania on October 15, 2026, affirmed dismissal of a constitutional challenge brought by Orchard BJK Company, LLC and Pocono Mountains Industries, Inc. against Coolbaugh Township's 2023 amendments tripling wetland buffer distances under its stormwater ordinance. The developers sued while their own petition asking the Township's Board to waive the new buffers for a planned warehouse on Lot 2 was still pending before the Board, and the court held the claims were not yet ripe because the Board could still grant the waiver.", "significance": "The ruling is a reminder to commercial and industrial landowners that courts will treat a constitutional challenge to a zoning or stormwater ordinance as premature when the ordinance itself preserves a waiver or variance mechanism and an application under that mechanism remains pending before the same body being sued. Developers should let a pending administrative waiver request play out, or affirmatively withdraw it, before suing over the underlying rule.", "judge": "Lori A. Dumas", "tags": ["zoning", "stormwater-ordinance", "warehouse-development", "ripeness", "pennsylvania"]}
